WEBVTT

00:00:00.000 --> 00:00:04.600
Captain Skyhawk, Soar Where No Man Has Gone Before.

00:00:20.160 --> 00:00:28.440
And welcome to NEStalgia, a chronological exploration of every NES game released in North America.

00:00:28.740 --> 00:00:30.320
I'm Mike. And I'm Sean.

00:00:31.080 --> 00:00:35.440
That's it for today, but that's okay because we have a topic right off the bat here.

00:00:35.780 --> 00:00:39.020
Captain Skyhawk, Soar Where No Man Has Gone Before.

00:00:39.260 --> 00:00:40.360
That's the back of the box.

00:00:40.520 --> 00:00:47.600
Is that a good claim for a video game when most video games also take place where likely no man has gone before?

00:00:48.460 --> 00:01:01.140
I like to think, and I think this is just, you know, how I was seeing things as a kid, like, to any particular video game, all other video games don't really matter.

00:01:01.380 --> 00:01:04.120
You know, like, to them, they're the only ones around.

00:01:04.260 --> 00:01:05.620
They're the only game in town.

00:01:05.740 --> 00:01:07.860
Or at least their marketing department thinks so.

00:01:08.000 --> 00:01:12.160
You know what I mean? Like, of course, it would be the place that No Man Has Gone Before.

00:01:12.160 --> 00:01:15.180
If you're going to space, no one's gone to space before.

00:01:15.180 --> 00:01:26.720
Right. And that's a great claim to make if you work for NASA's recruiting department and you're trying to get, like, you know, kids or college students, like, interested in, yeah, I want to go where No Man's Gone Before.

00:01:26.720 --> 00:01:31.600
But in a video game, I mean, like, you know, how many shmups do we play that take place in space?

00:01:31.620 --> 00:01:32.920
Like, I feel like I've been here.

00:01:34.120 --> 00:01:38.440
But have we been here in this perspective?

00:01:38.780 --> 00:01:51.000
Right. And with this particular plane in this particular vector in this particular neck of the woods localized entirely in your bedroom or living room, depending on where you had the console.

00:01:51.120 --> 00:01:52.140
Where did you keep your consoles?

00:01:52.500 --> 00:01:53.680
Oh, they were in the living room.

00:01:53.880 --> 00:01:56.800
I didn't have a TV in my room until I was, like, a teenager.

00:01:57.580 --> 00:02:02.520
Interesting. Okay, yeah, because I think we were a basement video game group, people.

00:02:02.700 --> 00:02:09.420
Like, our living room was really, it had a TV in it as well, but I guess my dad would have thought, like, that's for him to watch TV?

00:02:09.420 --> 00:02:11.500
Yes, yes. Okay, so, hold on.

00:02:11.740 --> 00:02:17.040
Early childhood, you know, when we had the Super NES, that was in the living room.

00:02:17.740 --> 00:02:21.580
Later childhood, that's when they kicked us down underground.

00:02:22.240 --> 00:02:23.300
Right, yeah, yeah, yeah.

00:02:23.360 --> 00:02:24.360
Get out of my way.

00:02:24.580 --> 00:02:25.440
Exactly. Okay.

00:02:26.820 --> 00:02:36.640
In Captain Skyhawk, which is not like an IP, by the way, Captain Skyhawk isn't, like, some cartoon or some recognizable comic book character.

00:02:36.680 --> 00:02:38.380
This is just the name of the game.

00:02:38.580 --> 00:02:45.040
And Captain Skyhawk is, I guess, like, a person piloting the vehicle.

00:02:45.180 --> 00:02:46.560
Who you never see. Yeah, you never see. The name of the plane.

00:02:46.720 --> 00:02:47.820
Yeah, you never see this person.

00:02:48.000 --> 00:02:58.840
I guess Captain Skyhawk is that guy on the front cover of the box who looks like Randy Quaid from Independence Day saying, like, you know, hello, boys, I'm back.

00:02:59.180 --> 00:03:00.980
Like, it's like, it has, like, that same expression.

00:03:01.220 --> 00:03:02.980
So, that's what I'm picturing in my head.

00:03:03.080 --> 00:03:10.680
Do we think that Independence Day sort of took liberties with IP from whoever designed this game?

00:03:11.180 --> 00:03:12.720
Okay, so, think about it, Sean.

00:03:12.820 --> 00:03:15.760
The plot for this game is that aliens have invaded Earth.

00:03:15.960 --> 00:03:17.280
And, I mean, stop right there.

00:03:17.440 --> 00:03:18.960
That is the plot to Independence Day.

00:03:19.080 --> 00:03:21.840
So, obviously, they ripped off Captain Skyhawk.

00:03:22.100 --> 00:03:23.820
But, it gets a little more interesting, okay?

00:03:24.260 --> 00:03:28.180
Aliens have invaded Earth, and they've built four land bases, okay?

00:03:28.320 --> 00:03:31.720
Like, if you want to get a kid hooked, you tell them that they've built land bases.

00:03:32.000 --> 00:03:34.060
Not, like, that they're probing people.

00:03:34.100 --> 00:03:37.600
Not that they plan to, like, disguise themselves as humans.

00:03:37.600 --> 00:03:39.340
No, they've built land bases.

00:03:39.340 --> 00:03:45.040
And these bases are designed to drain the Earth's energy and feed it to the mothership.

00:03:45.400 --> 00:03:52.420
If the mothership obtains enough energy, it will destroy the Earth with a massive laser blast.

00:03:52.500 --> 00:04:02.860
And I know we're talking about the plot of the game and everything, but I do have an issue with the way that's worded, because it makes it seem like a video game mechanic that you will see throughout this game.

00:04:02.860 --> 00:04:08.760
It's time. Yeah, that is, like, you know, there's an energy meter that, like, you basically need to be keeping track of.

00:04:08.920 --> 00:04:12.840
Like, whatever that thing in Mass Effect 3 was that you had to, like, always have in the back.

00:04:13.020 --> 00:04:15.320
Readiness? Yeah, like, what's my readiness?

00:04:15.840 --> 00:04:16.820
Galactic readiness, yeah.

00:04:17.180 --> 00:04:20.640
Yeah, like, you know, I don't want to be able to monitor that stuff, so I'm glad they didn't do that.

00:04:20.680 --> 00:04:23.540
But it does sound like that, based on the wording in the plot.

00:04:23.540 --> 00:04:30.400
So, you, the player, must destroy the four enemy bases, and then eventually go after the mothership itself.

00:04:30.980 --> 00:04:32.740
So, basically, Independence Day.

00:04:32.880 --> 00:04:45.160
Yeah. I think this would have been more okay and less of, like, a peeve of, like, well, they're kind of, this sounds like the story was written in the manual, like, completely separated from the game itself.

00:04:45.160 --> 00:04:52.360
But I think it would be less of a peeve if there was, like, if there was a little bit of banter between these missions.

00:04:52.640 --> 00:05:00.120
Because, like, it's not just go destroy the base, come back, refuel, go destroy the base, come back, refuel.

00:05:00.320 --> 00:05:09.760
Like, there's other stuff that you're doing, like picking up scientists and, like, resupplying other stuff on the, you know, on Earth.

00:05:09.760 --> 00:05:18.520
And if there was just, like, some characters that, like, there's a reason that this specific guy got kidnapped, I think it would make more sense.

00:05:18.680 --> 00:05:26.120
And then this whole, like, race against time, while it might not be mechanically important, would be, like, part of the story, at least.

00:05:26.380 --> 00:05:31.680
Yeah, and I think it does need just a little bit of Metal Gear-style cutscenes.

00:05:32.100 --> 00:05:40.620
Exactly, yeah. You know, or just, like, just some extra world building here, because you do get different scenarios in the game, and the objectives are read out on screen.

00:05:40.840 --> 00:05:46.940
But otherwise, you know, I don't know where we are on the Earth.

00:05:47.720 --> 00:05:53.540
You know, like, occasionally you'll see, like, a sphinx in the desert, and it's like, all right, they just don't have those everywhere, guys.

00:05:53.720 --> 00:05:55.640
Like, you know, like, those were specifically placed.

00:05:55.820 --> 00:05:58.900
The great, specifically placed.

00:05:59.480 --> 00:06:00.480
By the aliens.

00:06:01.820 --> 00:06:04.860
This is the great gridded plains of Egypt.

00:06:05.820 --> 00:06:08.680
You know that area of Egypt where everything's sort of gridded?

00:06:09.640 --> 00:06:11.000
Yes, absolutely. I think that's true.

00:06:11.000 --> 00:06:12.780
Well, actually, that's what it looks like from far enough away.

00:06:12.920 --> 00:06:22.300
You know, like, how, like, when you fly over rural America, it does look like, wow, so we just, like, marked off all the territory in squares, but really that's just how farming works?

00:06:24.080 --> 00:06:25.140
I did. Anyway.

00:06:25.300 --> 00:06:26.400
It did take me a while to know that. Yeah, go ahead.

00:06:27.500 --> 00:06:29.640
Say, it is not really a shmup.

00:06:29.640 --> 00:06:36.140
I don't want people to think that this game is a shmup like Gradius or any kind of horizontal or vertical shmup.

00:06:36.140 --> 00:06:48.640
This is more like a isometric shooter game and then, like, suddenly becomes a Top Gun style fighter shooter game, you know, for specific scenarios.

00:06:48.640 --> 00:06:57.500
So, to focus just on the isometric one, it's top down, but again, isometric, so you're kind of like at that 45 degree angle.

00:06:57.740 --> 00:07:05.480
Everything, as Sean hinted at, is, like, polygon-ish, or at least it has the implication of some 3D effect.

00:07:05.480 --> 00:07:10.540
So, there is a altitude matters in a game where you're controlling a ship.

00:07:10.720 --> 00:07:19.860
And sometimes we've seen an altitude radar for games that take place in space, and it really doesn't really matter for much other than just making sure that you're lined up with the enemy.

00:07:19.860 --> 00:07:28.360
But in this game, you actually have to be concerned with lining up and also dodging terrain as you're going through.

00:07:28.480 --> 00:07:37.780
So, I think, like, the actual environments themselves add some character, whereas in other non-isometric shmups, it's either just, like, just don't run into other pixels.

00:07:37.920 --> 00:07:38.920
Like, other pixels bad.

00:07:39.160 --> 00:07:41.980
This is more like, well, you have to think about where you are on the plane.

00:07:41.980 --> 00:07:46.060
And I think that's a pretty cool way of handling a shooter.

00:07:47.060 --> 00:07:48.700
There's a lot to unpack here.

00:07:48.920 --> 00:07:56.740
So, one, I would say that this is a shmup, just with some quirks in how it presents itself and controls.

00:07:59.080 --> 00:08:11.640
And, two, the amount of interaction that you're having with the actual landscape.

00:08:12.080 --> 00:08:20.000
Like, yes, it does present some interesting decisions to make, but you could also just, like, not make any and just stay as high as you can.

00:08:20.060 --> 00:08:24.680
Because otherwise, this game is kind of hard to parse.

00:08:25.400 --> 00:08:34.140
I don't know how you felt when you were controlling this thing to start and if maybe that experience changed as you were playing.

00:08:34.360 --> 00:08:47.560
But the whole, like, isometricness of this game does kind of, it's, the implementation feels very off when you first start playing it.

00:08:47.660 --> 00:08:50.620
Would you agree? Yes, because there's two big issues.

00:08:50.620 --> 00:08:53.820
And, you know, I was trying to just be kind to the game and set it up.

00:08:53.920 --> 00:08:55.520
But there's two big issues.

00:08:55.540 --> 00:08:59.620
And that is the field of view in this isometric thing is very narrow.

00:09:00.280 --> 00:09:02.460
So, you're only seeing, like, maybe enough.

00:09:02.680 --> 00:09:10.280
You're only registering enough to see, like, maybe two enemies and a specific hallway of your range at a time.

00:09:10.280 --> 00:09:20.420
And then the other problem is, so you're not seeing much, but the scroll speed is too fast for what you're able to see.

00:09:20.560 --> 00:09:27.320
And so you're just kind of dealing with things quickly or, as Sean's mentioning, just don't deal with them at all.

00:09:27.320 --> 00:09:41.980
Like, choose to just fire and take care of things if you can, but mostly just avoid because you don't want to have to, you know, it'd be cool to fly through, like, little caves or little entryways and stuff like that.

00:09:42.100 --> 00:09:47.900
But not only do those not exist in this game, but that would just make it harder because it's another thing to keep track of.

00:09:48.000 --> 00:09:55.180
Whereas, really, it's just hard enough to keep track of where you, the enemies, and both of your projectiles are at any given time.

00:09:55.180 --> 00:10:01.500
Yeah, and I couldn't really tell what changing your altitude actually did for you.

00:10:01.740 --> 00:10:07.740
Like, because I just kept pulling up on the D-pad, or I guess pushing down to pull up.

00:10:08.260 --> 00:10:17.740
And if I was just using my cannon, like, I could hit anything, whether it was sort of presenting itself as being on the ground or another, like, flying thing.

00:10:17.920 --> 00:10:19.520
Like, I was still hitting.

00:10:20.080 --> 00:10:25.880
I wasn't too sure about that, but that is definitely true of the bases, which makes it really silly, right?

00:10:26.000 --> 00:10:33.920
Yeah. Like, the bases should be the one where, like, it really does matter that you have to be low to the ground to take them on because otherwise...

00:10:33.920 --> 00:10:36.080
And, you know, they can hit you, too, so it doesn't really matter.

00:10:36.080 --> 00:10:39.560
But otherwise, what's the point of maneuvering?

00:10:39.660 --> 00:10:49.080
Other than, of course, the fact that because the game is isometric, it puts on this whole, you know, as we've said on the show before, pseudo-3D aspect to the game.

00:10:49.200 --> 00:10:50.840
That makes it all very cool, right?

00:10:50.860 --> 00:10:59.100
There is a presentationally cool thing about the way that this ship moves very fluidly and the altitude being convincing.

00:10:59.100 --> 00:11:09.080
Like, you can tell whether you're high above the ground or low to it, not just because of the shadow, but also just because of the way that the textures work on screen and everything.

00:11:09.240 --> 00:11:16.840
Like, it definitely sells flying a ship better than, you know, better than any of the 2D shmups that we play.

00:11:16.840 --> 00:11:19.600
Yeah, I'd give a hard agree there.

00:11:21.180 --> 00:11:29.420
Aside from the fact that, like, the only thing that you can really guarantee if you fly low to the ground is that you will probably crash into the ground.

00:11:29.620 --> 00:11:33.140
So, you may as well not really interact with that system.

00:11:33.480 --> 00:11:37.160
Is the goal ever as a plane to fly low to the ground, though?

00:11:37.480 --> 00:11:40.720
You know, it could be cool if you can control it.

00:11:40.720 --> 00:11:51.380
But, you know, the way that the arteries of the field flow sometimes, it can be kind of a pain in the ass.

00:11:51.560 --> 00:12:01.080
But, going with what you said of, like, how this kind of sells speed and flying better than most games that we've played so far.

00:12:01.080 --> 00:12:09.540
I think there's no better illustration of this than, I don't remember, I think this might be, like, the third level and then the sixth level.

00:12:10.860 --> 00:12:15.560
Where you actually have another, like, ship.

00:12:15.880 --> 00:12:17.120
Not a ship. It's a plane.

00:12:17.300 --> 00:12:19.740
This is just, like, a stealth fighter or something.

00:12:21.700 --> 00:12:27.380
Another plane that you're sort of dogfighting against that's sort of traveling near the same speed.

00:12:27.380 --> 00:12:30.360
And it kind of feels like a chase that you'd see in a movie.

00:12:30.360 --> 00:12:35.300
And, like, the fact that you guys are kind of going neck and neck and you're chasing him down.

00:12:35.460 --> 00:12:43.900
Like, that was probably, like, the most tense I've felt in, like, a shmup in terms of just, like, that presentational speed.

00:12:44.660 --> 00:12:45.740
That was really cool.

00:12:46.080 --> 00:12:49.620
Yeah, I think that's the closest video games have gotten to feeling like Top Gun.

00:12:50.120 --> 00:12:52.440
Yeah. You know, like, that actually felt...

00:12:52.440 --> 00:13:03.520
Right. And speaking of, there is a whole other type of plane combat in this game that plays very much like the dogfighting that we saw in the Top Gun games.

00:13:03.520 --> 00:13:11.160
Where, you know, your ship is just kind of always present in the, you know, in the front and center.

00:13:12.140 --> 00:13:14.480
You're facing... The camera's facing behind the plane.

00:13:14.660 --> 00:13:20.340
And then these other planes kind of fly in and ahead of you in their field of view.

00:13:20.500 --> 00:13:25.180
And the only thing I thought was weird about that, before we get into, like, whether that controlled any different than Top Gun,

00:13:25.380 --> 00:13:29.820
is that that's not really, like, the goal of other enemy planes.

00:13:29.820 --> 00:13:30.820
Like, right?

00:13:30.980 --> 00:13:33.640
Like, other enemy planes should be shooting from behind you, too.

00:13:33.800 --> 00:13:36.700
And there's no system for that.

00:13:36.880 --> 00:13:40.920
You know, they just are always advancing in front of you just to become easy targets.

00:13:41.480 --> 00:13:45.380
And, yeah, they shoot backwards, or at least some of them do.

00:13:45.700 --> 00:13:46.920
But it just...

00:13:46.920 --> 00:13:52.420
It felt like an extra minigame because it was so inconsequential that, like, nobody is technically chasing you.

00:13:52.580 --> 00:13:54.180
You're just chasing everybody else.

00:13:54.800 --> 00:13:56.300
That, like, why is this happening?

00:13:56.360 --> 00:13:57.940
Why is the enemies so dumb?

00:13:58.400 --> 00:14:02.820
Yeah. So, I agree that this does feel kind of like an afterthought.

00:14:03.020 --> 00:14:06.060
Though I would say that, again, it does look pretty.

00:14:06.060 --> 00:14:12.340
And it does feel like a good change of pace compared to, like, the standard game.

00:14:13.840 --> 00:14:16.320
But aside from...

00:14:16.840 --> 00:14:21.140
So, yeah, planes will approach from behind you and from in front of you.

00:14:21.140 --> 00:14:33.520
And aside from some that approach from behind you shooting missiles that are not really easy to dodge, but they're telegraphed well enough that unless you get really unlucky, you're not going to get hit by them.

00:14:35.440 --> 00:14:37.640
Yes, they're not really attacking you.

00:14:37.700 --> 00:14:46.780
They're just targets. I think it's just, like, sort of getting enough points to interact with the economy of the game and to switch it up a bit.

00:14:46.860 --> 00:14:50.680
So, it's not perfect, but it's fun enough.

00:14:51.180 --> 00:14:57.700
Yeah, and I think also that's my distinction of why I wouldn't just call this a shmup because it has switching objectives.

00:14:58.540 --> 00:15:02.380
Whereas shmups are almost always just get to the end of...

00:15:02.380 --> 00:15:06.300
Survive to the end of the stage and kill probably a boss.

00:15:06.300 --> 00:15:26.020
This has specific scenarios such as, you mentioned, you know, pick up the scientist, drop supplies into specific areas, drop zones, destroy the enemy planes, whether you're doing the dogfighting part or whether you're chasing them in the isometric parts.

00:15:26.020 --> 00:15:38.220
Like, it shifts enough what the goal is that, like, I hear what you're saying, sure, but, like, shmup as a genre versus, like, shmup as a trope might be, like, where this differentiates, right?

00:15:38.680 --> 00:15:40.780
Okay, yeah. Like, this doesn't have the tropes.

00:15:40.800 --> 00:15:45.740
I'd say that it is a...

00:15:46.240 --> 00:15:49.060
It's wearing a lot of the same clothes as a shmup.

00:15:49.560 --> 00:16:16.180
Okay. The plane codenamed the F-14 VTS, which is a fictional version of the F-14.

00:16:17.380 --> 00:16:19.380
What does the VTS stand for, then?

00:16:19.580 --> 00:16:22.640
Like, why not just make it, like, the F-99 or something, then?

00:16:22.760 --> 00:16:25.680
Like, you know, go really high, pie-in-the-sky ideas.

00:16:26.020 --> 00:16:28.820
The VTS, like, you got any ideas there?

00:16:29.000 --> 00:16:39.880
It almost sounds like they're trying to do, like, a VTOL thing because, like, you can't fight a base on the ground unless you can hover, right?

00:16:40.000 --> 00:16:41.960
So maybe they're trying to make it...

00:16:41.960 --> 00:16:46.980
They didn't know what the actual acronym was, so instead of VTOL, they did VTS, you know?

00:16:47.520 --> 00:16:50.660
Yeah, and I'm sure that was covered in the manual as well.

00:16:50.740 --> 00:16:53.760
Actually, no, it wasn't. It just says VTS.

00:16:53.760 --> 00:16:56.220
It doesn't tell you what the VTS does.

00:16:56.840 --> 00:16:57.980
Yeah, that's pretty silly.

00:16:58.500 --> 00:17:05.920
That stinks. All right, so, you know, we talked about how the plane controls and everything, and it is weird to call this, like, a plane.

00:17:06.040 --> 00:17:08.900
Like, it's not a commercial airliner, but it definitely, like...

00:17:08.900 --> 00:17:12.060
Well, would you call, like, a jet fighter a ship?

00:17:12.800 --> 00:17:13.960
Right. No, you're right.

00:17:14.360 --> 00:17:24.420
But I guess what I mean is, is, like, it's kind of dull-looking because I feel like it has to be based on the, you know, the power of the NES, right?

00:17:24.520 --> 00:17:27.840
Like, there's something about the fluid animation and the way this thing...

00:17:27.840 --> 00:17:33.880
Like, it's just gray, and it's just a couple different shades to communicate, like, you know, some depth on it.

00:17:34.160 --> 00:17:36.580
Otherwise, it's not, like, an impressive-looking ship.

00:17:37.680 --> 00:17:38.840
You know, you're right.

00:17:38.840 --> 00:17:42.840
But the vibe, especially of this...

00:17:43.700 --> 00:17:47.440
I don't even know what we're calling, like, the bonus mode, I guess.

00:17:47.600 --> 00:17:51.900
The, you're just flying, there's no isometric stuff going on.

00:17:53.380 --> 00:18:01.060
You wouldn't know that you're fighting aliens because it seems like they have the same sort of 1980s-era technology that we have.

00:18:02.460 --> 00:18:08.660
So, it at least is keeping things consistent visually.

00:18:09.540 --> 00:18:16.440
Yes, you're right. In fact, Sean, I made note of that as well, that there is only supernatural things going on otherwise.

00:18:16.660 --> 00:18:18.120
Like, there's no alien notes.

00:18:18.220 --> 00:18:21.820
There's just, okay, they made the pyramids able to shoot fireballs.

00:18:21.820 --> 00:18:24.080
The Sphinx are able to fight back now.

00:18:24.220 --> 00:18:25.560
Like, is that something...

00:18:25.560 --> 00:18:26.900
Were those always aliens?

00:18:27.240 --> 00:18:29.920
Maybe. Or do they control those?

00:18:29.920 --> 00:18:30.780
Like, yeah, that's what...

00:18:30.780 --> 00:18:34.100
Actually, see, that's what I'm talking about. Going back to that idea of, like, they placed the Sphinxes.

00:18:34.480 --> 00:18:39.780
Like, this is, like, that War of the Worlds type stuff where, like, they were always underground and then...

00:18:39.780 --> 00:18:44.460
Buried there. Yeah, but just the machines were buried there and now the aliens have come back.

00:18:45.400 --> 00:18:47.300
I would have appreciated more of that.

00:18:47.880 --> 00:18:55.360
Instead, what we're getting feels like, and this is a rare game, we're getting, like, some recycled graphics from other rare games.

00:18:55.500 --> 00:18:56.540
This is a rare game?

00:18:56.800 --> 00:18:58.700
Yeah, it's a rare game, believe it or not.

00:18:59.280 --> 00:19:04.840
Interesting. Milton Bradley wants you to think that they made this game because it says Concept by Milton Bradley.

00:19:05.460 --> 00:19:09.360
And, you know, I don't have a developer diary here, so who knows how much was shared.

00:19:09.520 --> 00:19:18.640
But usually when you're the publisher, that just means that you, you know, you put forward the costs and the developer actually made everything that the game had to offer.

00:19:20.640 --> 00:19:22.320
So, you know, take it for what you will.

00:19:22.320 --> 00:19:30.120
But, yeah, I'm having a tough time buying into anything that the game is putting down.

00:19:30.120 --> 00:19:38.460
But I'm also having enough fun just controlling the F-14 and completing the objectives.

00:19:38.860 --> 00:19:49.600
Like, even the, what was really hard in Top Gun, landing the plane on the carrier, they have a similar thing here where you have to dock the F-14 into a space station.

00:19:51.400 --> 00:19:56.120
And, thankfully, they don't make it like, okay, but if you crash the plane, it's game over.

00:19:56.120 --> 00:19:57.420
There's no time limit.

00:19:57.600 --> 00:20:00.840
You can just sort of fiddle it in there.

00:20:01.620 --> 00:20:05.940
Yeah. But, again, it's just like, it's another thing that I'm just like, oh, wow, another change.

00:20:06.080 --> 00:20:09.900
You know, like, not that the whole game always surprises you or anything.

00:20:09.900 --> 00:20:12.220
That would have been welcomed, of course.

00:20:12.380 --> 00:20:20.780
But it's not just like, all right, another shmup clone where all I'm doing is just, the hook is that it's isometric.

00:20:21.020 --> 00:20:26.040
Like, this game went well and beyond just a new graphics approach.

00:20:26.340 --> 00:20:27.260
Yeah, absolutely.

00:20:27.260 --> 00:20:31.100
And, like, just that thing about surprises that you said.

00:20:31.240 --> 00:20:35.240
Like, they actually do keep doing that because, you know, you get into that flow.

00:20:35.240 --> 00:20:40.220
You get into that rhythm of, like, okay, I'm going to do something in ISO space.

00:20:40.220 --> 00:20:43.120
Then I'm going to do something in fly forward space.

00:20:43.280 --> 00:20:44.940
Then I'm going to dock my plane.

00:20:45.060 --> 00:20:46.600
And you're just going to keep doing that.

00:20:46.600 --> 00:20:48.240
I'm going to buy some more missiles.

00:20:48.260 --> 00:20:49.920
I'm going to buy some more bombs.

00:20:50.140 --> 00:20:59.360
And then, finally, after one of the last levels, you think you're just going back to the space station to dock.

00:20:59.360 --> 00:21:06.560
And now you've basically got the Omega to your Alpha, like another space station.

00:21:06.760 --> 00:21:08.200
But now you've got to blow it up.

00:21:08.520 --> 00:21:17.120
And whether or not it's a satisfying end of the game after you blow it up is kind of beside the point.

00:21:17.200 --> 00:21:21.500
But the fact that, like, you kind of thought it was going to go this way.

00:21:21.600 --> 00:21:23.320
And then you're like, oh, it's another, like, boss.

00:21:23.500 --> 00:21:24.480
That was pretty cool.

00:21:24.600 --> 00:21:30.220
I like that. But how interesting are the other objectives of the game?

00:21:30.440 --> 00:21:34.180
Like, we were talking about picking up the scientist or dropping the supplies.

00:21:35.420 --> 00:21:37.140
It remains to be seen.

00:21:37.860 --> 00:21:46.300
Or at least we haven't commented yet on whether those things are actually fun or if they just provide something other than shoot all the enemies.

00:21:47.040 --> 00:21:56.580
With the scientist one, it's really just a re-skinned boss, which has, like, a little epilogue, if you want to call it that.

00:21:57.720 --> 00:22:03.580
Because it's still like, oh, blow up these four things, and then you beat the boss.

00:22:03.640 --> 00:22:09.480
But now, blow up these four things, and then go land, and then pick up this little pixel dude.

00:22:11.040 --> 00:22:22.860
Whereas with the resupply missions, you kind of just, the level will end once you sort of complete it in the field.

00:22:22.980 --> 00:22:23.880
There's no boss.

00:22:24.780 --> 00:22:32.920
And it's really just like, can you time it right to get everything done and get the level over quickly?

00:22:32.920 --> 00:22:40.880
So I wouldn't say that the different objectives in the ISO space are really all that interesting.

00:22:40.880 --> 00:22:44.900
It's just at least it's not the same thing over and over.

00:22:45.080 --> 00:22:49.400
You get at least some visual changing things up.

00:22:49.600 --> 00:22:57.740
Between stages, when you're shooting down those enemy planes in the Top Gun-style dogfighting,

00:22:57.740 --> 00:23:04.980
those are rewarded with points, which basically points translate into money in this economy

00:23:04.980 --> 00:23:09.240
to purchase weapons and ammo, new weapons and ammo, I should add.

00:23:09.860 --> 00:23:13.480
Like, better ammo than just the unlimited firepower that you have.

00:23:13.520 --> 00:23:17.400
You can never run out of ammo entirely, but you can buy better ammunition.

00:23:18.300 --> 00:23:24.300
Truly a capitalist society that we live in, where Earth is being threatened by aliens,

00:23:24.300 --> 00:23:29.760
and we're still the only people who are, you know, like, we're the only defense left,

00:23:29.760 --> 00:23:32.440
and we still have to pay for the survival.

00:23:32.440 --> 00:23:35.000
So maybe it was all priced in.

00:23:35.120 --> 00:23:38.160
Maybe the alien invasions are already priced into the stock market.

00:23:38.160 --> 00:23:39.260
The invasion is priced in.

00:23:39.260 --> 00:23:43.740
I like that. It might be, because they feel comfortable charging us.

00:23:43.820 --> 00:23:47.740
So maybe actually all the, maybe we're buying from the aliens, for all we know.

00:23:49.360 --> 00:23:52.160
I get it from a systems thing.

00:23:52.160 --> 00:23:56.100
They should have just, like, they should have rewarded you with alien tech

00:23:56.100 --> 00:23:57.900
for blowing up the aliens, right?

00:23:58.060 --> 00:24:00.960
Like, that would have, obviously they would have to also look like alien ships,

00:24:00.980 --> 00:24:04.360
to Sean's earlier point, that, like, it doesn't feel like you're even fighting aliens.

00:24:05.020 --> 00:24:08.900
But I do think that this is a missed opportunity to be like,

00:24:09.520 --> 00:24:12.920
Earth's all in it together, except for these guys who make the ammunition.

00:24:12.960 --> 00:24:16.800
They don't care if we live or die, because I guess they'll have the ammunition.

00:24:16.800 --> 00:24:23.860
I guess, like, if you can head Canada away to just, like, oh, we're going to,

00:24:23.880 --> 00:24:28.120
I'm just going to look at this as we picked up enough supplies

00:24:28.120 --> 00:24:32.940
to then convert it into missiles and bombs,

00:24:32.940 --> 00:24:36.780
and you're not being charged to resupply your own ship.

00:24:36.880 --> 00:24:38.380
Because, like, they're called credits,

00:24:38.800 --> 00:24:41.980
and I know that that is 100% just, you know, money,

00:24:41.980 --> 00:24:46.520
but it's abstracted enough away that you can sort of,

00:24:46.980 --> 00:24:51.780
maybe your CO isn't really trying to rip you off.

00:24:52.180 --> 00:24:58.700
Yeah, it's like the, that's this universe's version of the MCU Tesseract stuff

00:24:58.700 --> 00:25:00.440
that just constantly just keeps being like,

00:25:00.680 --> 00:25:02.800
yep, we have more of this, not Tesseract,

00:25:02.920 --> 00:25:07.240
but the Kree stuff from the first Avengers film

00:25:07.240 --> 00:25:09.480
just continually just keeps showing up to be like,

00:25:09.480 --> 00:25:13.280
yep, we use this tech to make new weapons that created new supervillains.

00:25:13.540 --> 00:25:15.640
Ah, yes. You know, just always explaining it away.

00:25:15.820 --> 00:25:17.380
Like, yeah, remember that stuff from the first,

00:25:17.480 --> 00:25:21.560
it's like, yeah, we blew up those aliens and changed it into this stuff.

00:25:21.560 --> 00:25:24.600
So you have your regular cannon, you have,

00:25:24.800 --> 00:25:26.080
I'll give them the cool names,

00:25:26.380 --> 00:25:28.560
the Phoenix Air Intercept missiles,

00:25:28.980 --> 00:25:31.160
the Maverick air-to-ground missiles,

00:25:31.700 --> 00:25:33.120
and the Hawk bombs.

00:25:33.120 --> 00:25:34.400
And you can buy these,

00:25:34.880 --> 00:25:36.280
and they do come in limited supply,

00:25:36.280 --> 00:25:38.900
and they do also have fluctuating cost,

00:25:38.920 --> 00:25:40.360
not between levels,

00:25:40.460 --> 00:25:42.860
but just like, they don't all cost the same.

00:25:43.000 --> 00:25:46.680
So the missiles, then the intercept missiles,

00:25:46.880 --> 00:25:48.020
then the air-to-ground missiles,

00:25:48.020 --> 00:25:49.740
and the Hawk bombs all have,

00:25:49.760 --> 00:25:52.520
you know, slightly scaling up cost.

00:25:52.940 --> 00:25:54.940
And yeah, because one of them,

00:25:55.060 --> 00:25:58.840
when you're upgrading, when you're purchasing something for the cannon,

00:25:58.860 --> 00:26:01.460
you're not buying ammo for the cannon,

00:26:01.460 --> 00:26:02.760
you are upgrading the cannon.

00:26:02.760 --> 00:26:05.520
So you are getting a faster fire rate,

00:26:05.620 --> 00:26:07.520
and by the time that thing's fully upgraded,

00:26:07.520 --> 00:26:09.240
you can just hold the button down,

00:26:09.300 --> 00:26:11.400
instead of, like, mashing it.

00:26:13.300 --> 00:26:15.100
And I thought that was cool,

00:26:15.160 --> 00:26:16.620
that there was both, like,

00:26:16.680 --> 00:26:19.380
long-term and short-term purchases,

00:26:21.080 --> 00:26:22.900
because then you end up having to, like,

00:26:23.300 --> 00:26:25.200
you'll have to actually restock your missiles,

00:26:25.260 --> 00:26:26.120
and restock your bombs,

00:26:26.220 --> 00:26:28.980
but those cannon upgrades stay.

00:26:29.580 --> 00:26:30.580
That being said,

00:26:30.800 --> 00:26:32.380
I did find it kind of difficult

00:26:32.380 --> 00:26:34.400
to use all of these

00:26:34.400 --> 00:26:37.160
at, like, on the actual field.

00:26:37.460 --> 00:26:38.980
Like, I could, I know that, like,

00:26:39.180 --> 00:26:40.380
directional input mattered,

00:26:40.380 --> 00:26:41.840
so I was able to get, like,

00:26:41.960 --> 00:26:43.800
the sort of spitfire,

00:26:44.840 --> 00:26:45.680
like, expanding,

00:26:47.800 --> 00:26:48.640
like, projectile,

00:26:48.760 --> 00:26:50.100
and I was able to do,

00:26:50.220 --> 00:26:51.260
like, the cluster bomb,

00:26:51.320 --> 00:26:53.000
it just sort of hits everything out there,

00:26:53.040 --> 00:26:55.220
but I don't think I was able to use

00:26:55.220 --> 00:26:57.380
the actual, like, missile missile at all.

00:26:58.260 --> 00:26:59.500
Yeah, it's funny,

00:26:59.520 --> 00:27:01.420
because it's not directional,

00:27:01.840 --> 00:27:03.220
even though it feels like it,

00:27:03.260 --> 00:27:04.680
because there are other things in the game

00:27:04.680 --> 00:27:07.660
that are directional. You can barrel roll in this game,

00:27:07.660 --> 00:27:09.080
you can do a barrel roll.

00:27:09.980 --> 00:27:11.820
You can, with the weapons, though,

00:27:12.000 --> 00:27:14.720
it is about how it's pressed.

00:27:14.960 --> 00:27:17.320
So, for instance, the air-to-ground missiles,

00:27:17.860 --> 00:27:19.460
the manual describes it as just

00:27:19.460 --> 00:27:21.500
lightly tapping the B button,

00:27:21.720 --> 00:27:23.420
instead of a hard press.

00:27:23.560 --> 00:27:25.120
It really felt like I was,

00:27:25.120 --> 00:27:27.460
I was, okay, that makes sense.

00:27:27.720 --> 00:27:28.720
No, yeah, you believed it,

00:27:28.720 --> 00:27:30.640
so it became what it was,

00:27:30.720 --> 00:27:31.900
but, like, the bombs,

00:27:32.680 --> 00:27:34.420
you have to press the,

00:27:34.520 --> 00:27:35.820
you have to, like, hold and release

00:27:35.820 --> 00:27:36.740
the B button, right?

00:27:36.800 --> 00:27:40.160
So you have to do, like, a full push up and down

00:27:40.160 --> 00:27:41.600
on your, with your thumb,

00:27:41.780 --> 00:27:43.480
whereas the air-to-ground missiles

00:27:43.480 --> 00:27:44.660
is, like, a quick tap,

00:27:44.720 --> 00:27:45.680
and obviously, like,

00:27:45.720 --> 00:27:47.320
we know from all platformers

00:27:47.320 --> 00:27:49.160
that the NES can recognize the difference,

00:27:49.360 --> 00:27:50.880
which in itself is pretty novel,

00:27:50.880 --> 00:27:53.340
but we have not really seen

00:27:54.400 --> 00:27:57.400
this in many other kinds of games

00:27:57.400 --> 00:27:59.460
other than jump functionality, right?

00:27:59.520 --> 00:28:00.740
Like, I don't think we've ever seen

00:28:00.740 --> 00:28:03.000
how you shoot is based on,

00:28:03.160 --> 00:28:04.540
or, like, even just switching

00:28:04.540 --> 00:28:06.400
what type of ammo you use

00:28:06.400 --> 00:28:08.820
is based on how you press the A and B.

00:28:08.820 --> 00:28:09.980
It reminds me,

00:28:10.300 --> 00:28:12.100
and maybe this is an X thing,

00:28:12.320 --> 00:28:14.120
but did Mega Man have this?

00:28:14.940 --> 00:28:16.500
No, Mega Man made it

00:28:16.500 --> 00:28:18.620
so that you can,

00:28:18.640 --> 00:28:20.480
you switch with the select button,

00:28:20.500 --> 00:28:21.900
or the start button.

00:28:21.980 --> 00:28:22.840
I think it's the start button.

00:28:22.980 --> 00:28:24.540
So when I'm thinking of Mega Man

00:28:24.540 --> 00:28:26.960
charging up his piece here.

00:28:26.960 --> 00:28:28.100
Oh, you're thinking of a Mega Man game

00:28:28.100 --> 00:28:28.920
we haven't played yet.

00:28:29.080 --> 00:28:31.960
Oh, okay. The charge beam hasn't been introduced yet.

00:28:32.240 --> 00:28:33.100
Damn, okay.

00:28:33.840 --> 00:28:35.540
I feel like there was a game where you,

00:28:35.700 --> 00:28:37.420
I feel like there was a game

00:28:37.420 --> 00:28:38.760
that did charge a shot.

00:28:38.860 --> 00:28:40.920
I just can't remember it, but you're...

00:28:40.920 --> 00:28:42.440
No, you are right. There are definitely games

00:28:42.440 --> 00:28:43.840
we've played with a charge shot.

00:28:44.020 --> 00:28:45.300
Maybe even, it's been a while,

00:28:45.300 --> 00:28:47.640
but obviously most Metroid games have that.

00:28:47.760 --> 00:28:49.320
I'm not sure if the first Metroid game

00:28:49.320 --> 00:28:50.140
had that or not,

00:28:50.160 --> 00:28:52.180
but yes, definitely there has been games

00:28:52.180 --> 00:28:54.140
with a charge shot. Cool, cool.

00:28:54.380 --> 00:28:57.720
I have vague memories of things sometimes.

00:28:58.040 --> 00:28:59.920
On the sequels and spinoff side,

00:29:00.260 --> 00:29:04.100
no sequels for this game made by Rare.

00:29:04.420 --> 00:29:05.260
Is that surprising?

00:29:05.540 --> 00:29:08.860
Is that rare to see Rare not make a sequel?

00:29:10.920 --> 00:29:13.760
It's surprising because I feel like

00:29:13.760 --> 00:29:16.260
this game is solid enough to warrant one.

00:29:16.440 --> 00:29:17.880
I mean, I imagine the sales

00:29:17.880 --> 00:29:20.180
must just not have been there.

00:29:20.300 --> 00:29:23.240
Or Milton Bradley holds the IP rights

00:29:23.240 --> 00:29:24.360
for just the name.

00:29:26.300 --> 00:29:29.020
But I mean, I guess it's kind of surprising.

00:29:29.320 --> 00:29:31.120
It's also surprising just

00:29:31.120 --> 00:29:34.220
the Captain Skyhawk name, right?

00:29:34.220 --> 00:29:35.540
Like, I just,

00:29:36.180 --> 00:29:38.440
I think it feels kind of generic

00:29:38.440 --> 00:29:39.500
and throwaway.

00:29:39.500 --> 00:29:41.500
And that's also what might have surprised

00:29:42.160 --> 00:29:43.580
you from thinking like

00:29:43.580 --> 00:29:45.000
that this wouldn't be a Rare game,

00:29:45.140 --> 00:29:46.160
that they would have had like

00:29:46.160 --> 00:29:49.700
established NIP around their various games.

00:29:49.940 --> 00:29:52.100
And that Captain Skyhawk just feels like

00:29:52.100 --> 00:29:54.040
a game you would get from

00:29:55.120 --> 00:29:56.880
Tecmo or Victakai,

00:29:56.880 --> 00:29:58.380
not necessarily Rare.

00:29:59.320 --> 00:30:00.260
But then again,

00:30:00.460 --> 00:30:02.380
like Rare hasn't really been

00:30:03.380 --> 00:30:04.880
in Rare form

00:30:05.680 --> 00:30:07.540
like from the games that we've played.

00:30:07.740 --> 00:30:09.740
They kind of have bounced between

00:30:09.740 --> 00:30:11.140
like shovelware

00:30:11.140 --> 00:30:13.420
and kind of interesting games,

00:30:13.560 --> 00:30:14.640
haven't they? So they don't,

00:30:14.800 --> 00:30:16.080
like they're not really,

00:30:16.560 --> 00:30:18.240
they're not really known for building,

00:30:18.500 --> 00:30:19.880
like world building yet, right?

00:30:20.100 --> 00:30:22.240
Yeah, you're right. No, they're just an established name.

00:30:22.240 --> 00:30:23.360
It's kind of funny that,

00:30:23.500 --> 00:30:25.200
you know, we think of them

00:30:25.200 --> 00:30:27.180
for their good games,

00:30:27.240 --> 00:30:28.500
of which there are five,

00:30:28.700 --> 00:30:31.760
but we have 21 skip-its from Rare.

00:30:32.080 --> 00:30:34.360
Yeah. So that's a really bad ratio

00:30:34.360 --> 00:30:35.920
and we should probably just stop

00:30:35.920 --> 00:30:37.620
talking about them in such high regard

00:30:37.620 --> 00:30:40.640
when Konami manages to have

00:30:40.640 --> 00:30:41.940
five essential games

00:30:41.940 --> 00:30:42.920
and eight play-its

00:30:42.920 --> 00:30:44.340
out of 24 total games.

00:30:44.460 --> 00:30:45.860
Like that's much more impressive.

00:30:46.140 --> 00:30:46.960
Oh, absolutely.

00:30:47.240 --> 00:30:50.260
Rare's trash. Yeah, I'm never going to play

